\description{
Register a convergence callback or a prescribed-input declaration for the
current consumers or resource process. The declaration is stored in the model
together with the identity of its current dynamics provider. Replacing that
provider or changing the projection dispatch chain invalidates the
declaration until support is explicitly adopted again. Changing parameters
or consumer rate functions leaves the identity of the fish equations intact.
Calling \code{setProcess(params, process)} with no capability arguments explicitly
adopts the current provider for all retained capability families. A partial
patch adopts only the families explicitly supplied: convergence/role,
stationary/equation rates/tuning, reproduction level, transformations, or
mortality diagnostics, prescribed inputs, temporal dependence or resource
equations.
Omitted families keep their previous provider bindings, including staleness.
A replacement definition adopts all its supplied capabilities.
}
\details{
A convergence callback returns a data frame with the columns \code{measure},
\code{value}, \code{tolerance} and \code{units}. It supplies measurements, not verdicts;
\code{\link[=getSteadyAssessment]{getSteadyAssessment()}} compares each absolute value with its tolerance.
Callback arguments include the model, state, complete rate list, derivative,
time and process name; accept \code{...} for arguments not needed by the callback.

A new \code{definition} replaces the previous callback and role before explicit
arguments are applied. Without \code{definition}, omitted arguments are retained.
The default role is \code{"dynamic"}. A \code{"prescribed"} role declares the whole
state block as an input rather than a dynamic variable. A dynamic owner may
declare a partial \code{prescribed_mask_fun}. Supplying \code{convergence_fun = NULL}
removes its
callback. State and equations are not changed by this registration.
